## 1. Microlyte (A2005) — conservative care

Microlyte is an FDA 510(k)-cleared antimicrobial wound dressing (K153756) from Imbed Biosciences — an ultrathin, fully synthetic, conforming matrix with sustained ionic-silver activity (per the manufacturer's in vitro testing, 99.99% microbial reduction beyond 72 hours). Fully bioresorbable in about a week, no removal step, no refrigeration, no animal-derived components. It lets you manage bioburden and support healing in one product.

**Published evidence** (Microlyte platform — Matrix and SURGICAL configurations):
- **Chronic wounds** — *"Efficacy of a Bioresorbable Matrix in Healing Complex Chronic Wounds: An Open-Label Prospective Pilot Study."* Wounds. 2020;32(11):309–318. 32 patients / 35 wounds; 91% of wounds (29/32) healed or improved significantly by 12 weeks, 73% average area reduction.
- **SSI prevention** — Chatelain R. Wounds. 2021;33(10):245–252. Prophylactic Microlyte SURGICAL in at-risk diabetic foot/ankle surgery: no SSIs in the per-protocol group vs an ~13% historical baseline. Small, single-arm case series — read it in full before quoting it to a committee.

**Coverage under NGS.** Bills under **A2005** (per sq cm); applied with **CPT 15271–15278**. NGS has **no LCD** for skin substitutes/CTPs, so coverage runs on **§1862(a)(1)(A)** of the Social Security Act ("reasonable and necessary") — no mandated four-week failed-conservative-care window, no application cap. That's what makes it a legitimate early-intervention play, as long as the note carries it: (1) identify the contamination/bioburden risk, (2) state Microlyte was applied to reduce bioburden via sustained ionic-silver activity, (3) give the rationale for an antimicrobial matrix over a non-antimicrobial alternative, and (4) tie it to on-label use per the IFU and the cleared indication (K153756). The 510(k) and IFU are in the attached package for exactly that reason.

