## 2. Interfyl vs. Xcellistem — the non-sheet skin substitutes

I've got the new sizing guidance for Interfyl attached, plus the Xcellistem 510(k) and its studies. Headline: **they bill the same way — both are per-1-mg codes and both invoice at $50 per billable unit** — but they're built to do *opposite* things to the inflammatory phase. XcelliStem is designed to dissolve and provoke; Interfyl is designed to persist and quiet down.

| | **Xcellistem (A2004)** | **Interfyl (Q4171)** |
|---|---|---|
| **Source** | Porcine lung + spleen — xenograft | Human placental chorionic plate — allograft |
| **Regulatory** | 510(k) device, K172593, product code KGN | 361 HCT/P (21 CFR 1271) — no FDA clearance |
| **Form** | Dry powder, 250 / 500 / 1000 mg | Flowable gel 0.3–1.5 mL; particulate 50 / 100 mg |
| **Maker** | StemSys / FetTech (RTT, Bobber) | Celularity, distributed by Arthrex |
| **Billing unit** | per 1 mg | per 1 mg |

**1. Degradation kinetics — the core divergence.** Xcellistem is engineered to break down fast: the powder disintegrates and dumps matricryptic peptides, GAGs, and growth factors into the bed as a burst. Interfyl is meant to *stay* — it fills a soft-tissue deficit and remodels gradually as host cells migrate in.

That drives the infected-wound argument. Kimmel's poster makes it explicit: because the matrix is *supposed* to disappear, bacterial protease degradation isn't a failure mode, so you can apply it to a colonized bed immediately instead of waiting. Interfyl has no such argument — a scaffold whose job is to persist in dead space is exactly what you don't want to implant into infection.

**2. Immunomodulation — opposite aims.** Porcine ECM works partly *through* an inflammatory response: brisk macrophage recruitment, then an M1→M2 shift toward the constructive-remodeling phenotype. Sasse's 2-week biopsy showed that signature — remodeling neutrophil response, macrophage presence, neovascularization, no foreign-body granulomas. Interfyl markets the inverse: decellularized, not cross-linked, human — the pitch is *reduced* immunogenic/inflammatory response. The in vitro work supports it — Mao et al. (PubMed) found Interfyl attenuated pro-inflammatory cytokine, growth-factor, and MMP expression while raising TGF-β3 relative to competing placental matrices. TGF-β3 over TGF-β1 is the scar-free-healing axis, so that's a mechanistically real claim, not just marketing.

Net: **Xcellistem is trying to restart a stalled wound; Interfyl is trying to rebuild soft tissue without stirring up fibrosis.**

**3. Composition.** Xcellistem's differentiator claim is a deliberately low collagen fraction — ~45% collagen, 25% elastin, 14% fibronectin, 7% laminin, vs ~90% collagen for single-tissue ECMs — i.e., more integrin-binding ligand diversity per mg. Lung and spleen were chosen for that (lung is elastin- and basement-membrane-rich; spleen is dense vascular reticular tissue). Interfyl has the same general cast (collagen, elastin, fibronectin, laminins, GAGs) but positions on scaffold quality and immunologic quietness rather than growth-factor payload.
> ⚠️ **Caveat:** those Xcellistem percentages are from a **manufacturer poster, not independent characterization**. Do not put them in anything customer-facing as if they were third-party verified.

**4. Indication — the sharpest legitimate edge.** Xcellistem has cleared Indications for Use naming DFU, VLU, pressure ulcers, and burns. Interfyl, as a 361 HCT/P, has **no cleared indication at all** — its language is homologous-use framing ("replace or supplement damaged or inadequate integumental tissue," "fill irregular spaces or soft-tissue deficits"). A 361 product legally can't claim to treat a disease. If you're ever positioning these head-to-head, that asymmetry is Xcellistem's strongest honest argument.

